# The integration modules that do not build against the framework version their own go.mod pins, one line
# per module.
#
#   module ~ class ~ symbols ~ reason
#
# Read by .dev/validate/compatibility.sh. This is not a suppression list: every line is a claim the run
# has to satisfy. A module that fails with no line here fails the check, and a line whose module now
# builds fails it just as loudly — the break it excuses is gone, so the line has to go. That second half
# is what makes the release train visible: raising a pin repairs the module, and this band then demands
# the row be deleted rather than letting it rot into a permanent excuse for a module nobody builds.
#
# The symbols are the exact set the run measures, not a prefix of it. A row that named a subset would
# grow silently to cover the next symbol the branch starts using, which is what a wildcard would do spelled
# differently — so wildcards are refused and the set is compared for equality. When a module starts using
# one more symbol the pinned version lacks, its row is rewritten; that is the row doing its job.
#
# The declared pin is deliberately not a field. The run reads it from the go.mod and prints it beside the
# module, so this file cannot carry a version that has gone stale against the module it describes.
#
# The class is measured, not asserted, and the run refuses a row whose class no longer matches:
#
#   ahead     every diagnostic is an absence — the integration names something the pinned version does not
#             have, because the framework grew it on this branch and the pin still points at an older tag.
#             The dependency policy pins the oldest version that compiles, so this is the normal state of
#             an unreleased branch and the release train resolves it by raising the pin. Not a defect.
#
#             A row vouches for every package of its module, not only for the one go build stopped at: go build
#             checks none of the importers of a failing package, so the band type-checks a failed module again,
#             whole, through .dev/validate/typecheck, which reads an importer against the partial package it
#             imports. The run prints how many of the module's packages were type-checked and refuses a module
#             whose count falls short of its package count.
#
#   contract  a name that EXISTS at the pinned version changed shape, and the integration no longer
#             satisfies it. Raising the pin does not answer it: a third party implementing the same
#             interface stops compiling too. Every such row is a backwards-incompatible change on the
#             framework's published surface and needs a decision before it ships.
#
# Fields are trimmed, so the columns may be aligned. The reason is mandatory: a line whose reason is empty
# is a line nobody can review.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# the one contract break
#
# Measured against the pinned tag rather than argued: at that tag `Transport.Close(Runtime) error` had no
# production caller anywhere in the tree — the only two call sites were in the in-memory transport's own
# test — while the container's ordered teardown recognises `Close() error` and nothing else, so the old
# signature left every transport structurally unreachable at shutdown and a broker connection lived as
# long as the process. The change is therefore a defect repair whose remedy is not backwards compatible:
# nothing that worked breaks, because the door was dead, but a third-party Transport implementation stops
# compiling. The decision has been taken: it ships on v3.14.0, and the changelog and the upgrade guide both carry it. The row stays until that release raises the pin, which is what makes it disappear.


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# source ahead of the pinned tag
#
# Every row below is one class of fact: the branch added a symbol to the framework or to a sibling
# integration, the module started using it, and the pin still names a tag that predates it. The release
# train raises these pins, the modules build, and this band then demands every row here be deleted. A row
# that survives a release is a module published against a version that cannot compile it.

